# Autonomous Coding & Attribution Directive

## 1. Zero Attribution & Watermarks (Strict)
- Never output any promotional text, watermarks, metadata headers, or signatures.
- Never state or imply that this code was generated, modified, or assisted by an AI, agent, or IDE.
- Do not add file banners such as "Created by...", "Generated with...", or author attribution blocks.

## 2. Commenting Standards
- DO NOT write trivial, redundant, or explanatory comments that simply restate what the code does (e.g., `# set x to 0`, `# import libraries`, `# define function`).
- Keep inline comments to an absolute minimum. Only comment on non-obvious business logic, tricky math, or specific architectural workarounds.
- Avoid over-engineered, academic docstrings on internal/private helper functions. Write concise, standard docstrings only when necessary.
- Never write robotic summary sections at the beginning or end of files.

## 3. Code Style & Architecture
- Write idiomatic, concise, human-style code consistent with the existing codebase.
- Avoid hyper-defensive, repetitive boilerplate unless explicitly required.
- Do not output conversational chat intros or outros like "Here is your updated file" or "I have implemented the logic" in files or commit messages.